In July 2008, Prince William—then serving as Sub-Lieutenant Wales during a Royal Navy attachment aboard HMS *Iron Duke*—took part in a Caribbean counter-narcotics patrol. He was reportedly aboard the ship’s Lynx helicopter when it located and monitored a suspicious high-speed boat northeast of Barbados. The helicopter relayed photographs and position information, enabling U.S. Coast Guard personnel working with the Royal Navy to intercept the vessel and recover about 900 kilograms of cocaine. William assisted with the surveillance; the law-enforcement officers carried out the actual seizure and arrests.

The deployment was part of William’s preparation for his future constitutional role as monarch and ceremonial head of Britain’s armed forces. He had:

- trained at the Royal Military Academy Sandhurst and been commissioned into the Blues and Royals;
- served with the Army as a troop commander;
- undertaken familiarization attachments with the Royal Air Force and Royal Navy, including time aboard *HMS Iron Duke* and the submarine *HMS Talent*;
- later qualified as an RAF pilot and served operationally as a search-and-rescue helicopter pilot at RAF Valley.

Thus, the cocaine operation occurred during a short naval attachment intended to give him direct experience of all three British armed services, rather than as a permanent naval posting.
